  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Waterborne diseases outbreak anticipatory action and response to Harare Metro</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project will be implemented through a clear and complementary division of roles between Community Water Alliance (CWA) and Oxfam, ensuring efficient delivery of both community -level and technical interventions. CWA will lead community engagement, beneficiary targeting, and capacity -building activities, including door -to -door health and hygiene behaviour change campaigns, training of community health workers, Water Point Committee and Asset Management Committee training, mobilisation for roadshows and radio programmes, and support to flood -affected households and school -going children. Oxfam will provide technical leadership, hardware implementation, and coordination, including rehabilitation of community boreholes, installation of inline chlorinators, bulk water trucking, and training of health workers and district stakeholders on cholera case management, Integrated Phase Classification (IPC), surveillance, and anticipatory action programming. Oxfam will also oversee procurement, logistics, quality assurance, and coordination with government and Water Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH) sector platforms. This partnership ensures strong community ownership combined with high -quality technical delivery, enabling a timely, integrated, and accountable emergency WASH response in Harare Metropolitan.</narrative>
  </description>
